{
  "summary": "The manuscript reports a cross-sectional comparison of 284 self-identified ASMR participants (recruited from an ASMR Reddit forum) with 279 Qualtrics control participants who did not report ASMR. ASMR participants scored higher on the MAAS and on the Curiosity subscale of the TMS. Within the ASMR group, Curiosity correlated with average tingle intensity, and a sex difference in Curiosity was reported. The authors conclude that ASMR and mindfulness are related constructs, that ASMR may be a cognitively active process, and that mindfulness training could enhance ASMR. The group difference is plausible and modest in size. However, the design is correlational with non-equivalent recruitment, personality confounds are not addressed, the Decentering subscale was dropped after reliability problems, several reported degrees of freedom and sample sizes conflict, and the Discussion goes beyond what the data can support.",
