    {
      "claim": "The group difference in mindfulness is attributed to ASMR, but the two groups were recruited and screened differently and the authors' own prior openness finding was not controlled for.",
      "issue_type": "V2",
      "status": "missing_information",
      "severity": "major",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "input_field": "manuscript_text",
          "exact_quote": "Recruitment of participants with ASMR was conducted via an advertisement for the study",
          "location_hint": "Methods, Participants, lines 100-101 (p. 4)"
        },
        {
          "input_field": "manuscript_text",
          "exact_quote": "individuals with ASMR have higher levels of the openness-to-experience personality trait",
          "location_hint": "Discussion, lines 314-315 (p. 14)"
        },
        {
          "input_field": "page_images",
          "page": 7,
          "observation": "Lines 115-118 list the Big Five Inventory as part of the survey. Its scores are never entered as covariates in any analysis reported on pp. 9-13, although the authors cite openness as correlated with ASMR."
        }
      ],
      "reasoning": "ASMR participants were self-selected from an ASMR forum, while controls came from a commercial panel and were screened by watching videos. The ASMR group was not screened by video, and the ASMR group's self-report was not verified. The sex composition is also not identical as claimed: 135 of 284 ASMR participants were male (47.5%) and 123 of 279 controls were male (44.1%). Because openness, which the authors report is higher in ASMR, is closely tied to curiosity and mindful attention, the group effects on MAAS and Curiosity could partly reflect personality and recruitment differences. This weakens the central conclusion that ASMR and mindfulness are related. Covariate-adjusted analyses using the collected BFI data would be needed to address this."
    },
    {
      "claim": "Reported degrees of freedom and sample sizes are internally inconsistent across the text and Table 2, so the reported correlations and simple-effect tests cannot be verified.",
      "issue_type": "V3",
      "status": "confirmed_error",
      "severity": "major",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "input_field": "manuscript_text",
          "exact_quote": "r(563) = .234",
