        {
          "input_field": "page_images",
          "page": 26,
          "observation": "The Table 2 footnote states 'N = 173 ASMR participants with scores for all checklist items, MAAS items, and TMS - C items', and all correlations in the table, including MTotal with MAAS (.112) and TMS-C (.160), appear under this footnote. The text, however, reports the MTotal correlations with df = 284 and the MAAS-TMS-C correlation with df = 563."
        }
      ],
      "reasoning": "A correlation between two measures from at most 284 ASMR participants (the whole sample is 563 including controls, but the MAAS-Curiosity correlation was computed only in the ASMR group) cannot have df = 563. The MTotal correlations are described as using N = 284 in the text but N = 173 in Table 2. The simple-effects test for sex within the ASMR group reports F(1,559), which implies the error term from the full 563-person model, not the 284-person ASMR subsample. Because the sex-difference conclusion in the Conclusions rests on this test, the inconsistency directly affects that claim. The authors should state the exact n for each analysis and recompute the tests."
    },
    {
      "claim": "The Decentering subscale was dropped after reliability and factor-structure problems, and the Curiosity subscale, which is the basis of most conclusions, was retained despite showing the same kind of item cross-loading.",
