    {
      "claim": "The Discussion and Conclusions claim support for causal and mechanistic propositions (mindfulness training enhancing ASMR; sex-specific attentional versus cognitive mechanisms) that the correlational data cannot test.",
      "issue_type": "V8",
      "status": "confirmed_error",
      "severity": "major",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "input_field": "manuscript_text",
          "exact_quote": "The current study strongly supports the hypothesis that ASMR is related to mindfulness.",
          "location_hint": "Discussion opening, line 309 (p. 13)"
        },
        {
          "input_field": "manuscript_text",
          "exact_quote": "The current data suggest that it would, although additional research is necessary to",
          "location_hint": "Discussion, lines 366-367 (p. 16)"
        },
        {
          "input_field": "manuscript_text",
          "exact_quote": "suggest that males and females may experience ASMR in different ways, with males using",
          "location_hint": "Discussion, lines 347-349 (p. 15)"
        }
      ],
      "reasoning": "The study measured only self-reported state and trait mindfulness at one time point, with no training, no manipulation, and no measure of attentional or cognitive processing. Statements that data 'strongly support' a relation, that they 'suggest' training would enhance ASMR, and that males and females use different mechanisms go beyond the evidence. The sex difference is also based on a single interaction with p = .017 and the questionable df noted above. These claims should be reframed as hypotheses for future work, and the strength of the wording should match a cross-sectional study with group non-equivalence."
