  "overall_assessment": "The study addresses a reasonable question and reports its main group comparison with clear effect sizes. However, the central claim rests on a non-equivalent control group, with recruitment, screening and sex composition that differ from what is described, and with a known personality correlate (openness) that was measured but not controlled. Several reported degrees of freedom and sample sizes conflict across the text and Table 2, and the reliability problems with the Decentering subscale led to selective reporting of the Curiosity subscale. The Discussion and Conclusions make causal and mechanistic claims that the cross-sectional design cannot support. The paper would benefit from covariate-adjusted analyses, a full account of screening and attrition, corrected statistics, and substantially more cautious interpretation. The evaluation is limited to the supplied material; external data and code mentioned by the authors were not available."
